"tvfy_10711","person_slug":"sarah-hanson-young","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Scott Ludlam","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10713","person_slug":"scott-ludlam","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Scott Ryan","party":"PRES","person_id":"tvfy_10715","person_slug":"scott-ryan","vote":"no"},{"name":"Sean Edwards","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10756","person_slug":"sean-edwards","vote":"no"},{"name":"Simon Birmingham","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10044","person_slug":"simon-birmingham","vote":"no"},{"name":"Stephen Parry","party":"PRES","person_id":"tvfy_10507","person_slug":"stephen-parry","vote":"no"},{"name":"Sue Lines","party":"PRES","person_id":"tvfy_10782","person_slug":"sue-lines","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Zed Seselja","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10822","person_slug":"zed-seselja","vote":"no"}],"name":"Motions — Howard Government","noes":30,"question":"The majority voted against Labor senator [Anne McEwen's](https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/people/senate/sa/anne_mcewen) motion that the Senate remember the legacy of the Liberal [Howard Government](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Howard_Government), which she described negatively. The Liberal senator [Scott Ryan](https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/people/senate/victoria/scott_ryan) opposed the motion, saying that the Howard Government's legacy was good. ### Wording of the motion > That the Senate— >> (a) notes that 2 March 2016 marks the 20th anniversary of the election of the Howard Government in 1996; >> (b) recalls the record of the Howard Government, including: >>> (i) dogs on the waterfront, >>> (ii) the false claim that children of asylum seekers had been thrown overboard, >>> (iii) committing Australia to the war in Iraq on a false premise, >>> (iv) refusing to sign the Kyoto protocol, >>> (v) regional rorts, >>> (vi) wheat for weapons, >>> (vii) the absence of economic reform, >>> (viii) failing to support the transition of Australia to a republic, >>> (ix) refusing to say sorry to Indigenous Australians for past wrongs, >>> (x) its 10 point plan to amend native title legislation following the Wik decision, >>> (xi) spending over $1 billion on government advertising, >>> (xii) WorkChoices, and >>> (xiii) the erosion of decent standards in our public affairs; and >> (c) calls on all Australians not to forget the legacy of the Howard Government and its contribution to public policy in this country.","result":"negative","slug":"division-federal-senate-4129","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2016-03-02/15"}
